WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.550
Continental maps are snapshots of an evolving system, not photographs of

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:08.920
the distant past. This textbook presents one reconstruction beginning around six

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:11.950
hundred million years ago with Rodinia and its breakup.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:15.750
Its exact arrangement, including the neighbor once west of North America,

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:19.950
remains uncertain. Keep those source dates and uncertainties attached to the

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:23.970
sequence rather than treating every drawn coastline as precisely known.

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.660
By five hundred million years ago, North America and Europe were

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.600
separate, while Gondwana grouped the southern landmasses, including Africa, South America,

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:36.540
Antarctica, Australia, and India. Most of the rest of Earth was

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.180
occupied by a vast ocean.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:42.000
The modern continental names help identify pieces, but their positions and

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.600
orientations were very different.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:48.010
Around three hundred seventy million years ago, the reconstruction shows North

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.850
America joined with parts of Europe and approaching Gondwana.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:54.090
Collisions narrowed intervening oceans and built mountains.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.930
By the late Paleozoic snapshot around two hundred eighty million years

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.510
ago, the continents were assembled into Pangaea.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.970
The Appalachians and Ouachitas record parts of that assembly.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:07.090
The Tethys remained a major oceanic embayment.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.480
By the one-hundred-fifty-million-year snapshot, the Atlantic had begun separating North America

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.860
from Africa and South America.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.380
North America remained connected with Europe and Asia in Laurasia.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.960
Much of Gondwana was still joined, but further rifting would separate

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.520
South America from Africa and disperse its other pieces.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.020
Spreading creates new oceanic crust between moving continents;

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.320
it does not push them through older ocean floor.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.230
The present arrangement is another snapshot.

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:38.230
Work backward using matching rock histories and plate relationships, then forward

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:41.410
through the same sequence. The opening ocean and closing ocean are

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.490
linked to continental motions and mountain belts.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:47.390
Future arrangements require assumptions about how boundaries evolve;

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:51.430
today's directions alone cannot justify an exact distant-future map.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:56.130
Here, motion between supplied snapshots is a teaching schematic, not a

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:56.850
measured trajectory.
